+# Agent Note: Linux Desktop community packaging
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-10-27-linux-desktop-community-packaging.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The Desktop runtime already locks Linux x64 interpreters and native dependencies, but the packaging target list, Electron executable paths and packaged-runtime smoke assume macOS or Windows. Community distributors cannot produce a checked Linux application from the supported packaging entry point.
+
+## Decision
+
+Desktop accepts `linux-x64` as a packaging and development-build target on Linux x64 hosts. The target prepares the locked Linux runtime, uses Electron's Linux executable layout, installs the POSIX `dsh` launcher and verifies the assembled `linux-unpacked` application. electron-builder emits AppImage and DEB artifacts with the `deepseek-harness` executable name.
+
+Linux packaging is unsigned only. It uses the configuration-free local application ID, strips release settings, carries no mandatory-update policy or automatic-update feed, writes no release completion record and remains outside the official Desktop upload and retention target types. Artifact filenames retain the unsigned marker. The platform package formats are community distribution outputs; each distributor owns distribution-specific launch, sandbox, desktop-integration, native-dependency and upgrade qualification.
+
+The new target remains isolated under `.desktop-build/targets/linux-x64/`. Shared immutable downloads remain shared. Platform and architecture flow through runtime preparation, dsh preparation, electron-builder and packaged-runtime smoke as one selected target.
+
+This decision partially supersedes the Linux-target absence recorded in [the window-drag coverage note](../architecture/2026-09-19-window-drag-coverage-contract.md). That note remains active because its macOS drag ownership and test matrix still govern the shell.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Cross-build Linux artifacts on Windows.** Native runtime smoke and target-native dependencies require a Linux host; cross-building would weaken the existing packaging verification.
+
+**Add Linux to the official update feed.** The release service, signatures, retention inventory and installed-update qualification do not yet define Linux behavior. Keeping community artifacts outside those types prevents an unsigned package from being mistaken for an official update.
+
+**AppImage only.** A DEB gives Debian-family users package-manager installation and desktop integration while AppImage remains portable. Producing both from the same verified unpacked application does not duplicate runtime preparation.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Linux x64 CI can build two installable formats without signing credentials or release dotenv files. The artifacts are larger than a directory build and are not automatically updated. Linux support claims must name tested distributions and remain community-qualified until official signing, update, retention and installed-upgrade behavior are designed and verified.
